// Workload B for `nested_math.of`: the *same code*, driven differently.
//
// Metric G-13. Real deployments collect a profile on one workload and run the
// binary on another, so a speedup measured with the profile applied to the very
// run it came from is the best case and not the interesting one. These variants
// change only `main` -- every function the profile describes is character for
// character the one it was collected from -- so the profile still matches by
// name and the only thing that has shifted is what the program actually does.
//
// Workload A runs the inner loop 400 times per entry, 600,000 times over.
// Workload B runs it 3 times per entry, 80,000,000 times over. The product
// is 240,000,000 inner iterations either way, so the two do the same amount
// of arithmetic -- but a profile collected on A measures a trip count of 400
// and unrolls by 8, which on B is eight copies of a loop that runs three
// times.
fn kernel(int rows, int cols) -> int {
    int acc = 0;
    int r = 0;
    while (r < rows) {
        int c = 0;
        while (c < cols) {
            acc = acc + r * c - (r + c);
            c = c + 1;
        }
        r = r + 1;
    }
    return acc;
}
fn main() -> int { print_int(kernel(80000000, 3)); return 0; }
